<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;Show</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;Hide</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>Thinking about it more, I think the problem is that you're trying to do too much here. You've got the ISA artcile, you've got the structure and the logs of the other planet, which are very interesting in and of themselves. Then you've got this story based on half-dead MTF who are contemplating what its like to live forever, what its like to die repeatedly, and they didn't even die in or because of this skip, but in some previous tale somewhere. You're using this skip to tell the end of their story, but we don't know the beginning of their story without reading something else.</p>
<p>Skips should be VERY self-contained in my opinion. For instance, I shouldn't need to know who Dr. Wondertainment is before reading a skip that references Dr. Wondertainment. The skip should provide any relevant information. Which isn't to say a skip couldn't be -enhanced- by knowing the backstory of the GOI/characters involved, but it shouldn't be necessary.</p>
<p>I would honestly have separated this out into a skip and a tale, crosslinking them by putting a link in one of the names. If you rewrote some of the meat (but not ALL the reveals) of the exploration log as recorded by D-Class, or generic MTF, and then maybe included a link at the bottom of the article saying, &quot;Additional exploration logs can be found here,&quot; and that's where your story of undead MTF exploring this thing and contemplating their existence, then I think this would be more successful.</p>
<p>Obviously this skip is in no danger of being voted off the site, but I really think this could be a SUPER successful skip, like 100+ easily, if not for these problems.</p>
<p>But as always, this is just one man's opinion, take it with a grain of salt.</p>
</div>
